msgid "Menus"
msgstr ""

msgid "*Every command of the menus, where it appears and what it does.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The menu bar at the top of the window holds **File**, **Edit**, "
"**Window** and **Help**: the commands that belong to the whole study "
"rather than to one window. The same menus appear on the desktop and on "
"the web, because EMStudio draws them from one table of commands. On the "
"macOS desktop the system's menu bar carries them too, together with the "
"**EMStudio** menu of the Mac; its **Window** menu adds the Mac's own "
"Minimize, Zoom and Enter Full Screen. On Windows and Linux the menu bar "
"inside the window is the only one."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"There is no **Tools** menu any more. Each of its commands lives where it "
"serves: in the header of the window it works on, in ``File ▸ Bring in…``,"
" in ``Edit ▸ Clean up``, in the Settings, in **Window**. The table :ref"
":`menus-where-tools-went` says where each one went, and the command "
"search (:kbd:`F3`) finds every command by its name."
msgstr ""

msgid "The File menu open."
msgstr ""

msgid "The Edit menu open."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Window menu open: Workspaces, Scripting workspace, Python console, "
"Detach the active window, Bring back the detached windows, Maximise the "
"area, Area headers."
msgstr ""

msgid "The Help menu open."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The commands of a single window are elsewhere: in the window's own "
"header, in the menu that opens with a right click on the window, and in "
"the menu *This window's tools* at the right of the header. :doc:`windows`"
" describes them with each type of window."
msgstr ""

msgid "Find a command (F3)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``Edit ▸ Search commands…`` opens the command search: one field, and "
"under it the commands that answer what you type. Press :kbd:`F3`, or "
":kbd:`⌘K` (:kbd:`Ctrl+K` on Windows and Linux), from anywhere in the "
"window."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The command search open, «chronology» typed in its field: Check the "
"chronology… first, with where it lives, the Matrix header and the ▲ of "
"the status bar."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Type a part of the name. The search knows every command by its English "
"name and by its Italian name, whatever the language of the interface, and"
" by a few other words: *tools*, *strumenti*, *cronologia*, *colours*, "
"*console*…"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each result says where the command lives now (a menu, the header of a "
"window, a page of the Settings) and its keys, if it has any."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":kbd:`↑` and :kbd:`↓` choose a result, :kbd:`Enter` runs it, :kbd:`Esc` "
"closes the search. A second :kbd:`F3` closes it too."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A command that cannot run now (it needs a document, a selection, or a "
"window of its kind) says so instead of running."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The commands of the plugins installed on this computer come in by "
"themselves, with the name of their extension."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``Help ▸ Shortcuts`` lists the search with the other keys (:kbd:`F3` · "
":kbd:`⌘K`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Where the commands of Tools went"
msgstr ""

msgid "It was"
msgstr ""

msgid "It is now"
msgstr ""

msgid "``Tools ▸ Add node…``"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Add ▾** in the header of the **Matrix** (the same menu as :kbd:`⇧A`, "
"which still works)."
msgstr ""

msgid "``Tools ▸ Check the chronology…``"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Chronology** in the header of the **Matrix** (it shows **▲ n** when the"
" chronology has warnings), and the ▲ of the status bar: its pill counts "
"the warnings of the chronology apart (**▲ n chronology**), and one click "
"opens the check."
msgstr ""

#, fuzzy
msgid "``Tools ▸ StratiMiner``"
msgstr "StratiMiner"

msgid ""
"``File ▸ Bring in…``, its fourth choice: **A folder of unstructured "
"sources, with StratiMiner…**"
msgstr ""

msgid "``Tools ▸ Read as DosCo…``"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``File ▸ Bring in…``, the choice **A folder read as DosCo…**, and the "
"button of the empty **Shelf**."
msgstr ""

msgid "``Tools ▸ Normalise spellings…``"
msgstr ""

msgid "``Edit ▸ Clean up ▸ Normalise spellings…``, beside **Sort lanes by date**."
msgstr ""

msgid "``Tools ▸ Colours…``"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``Settings… ▸ Colours`` (it says which Space, and opens its window), and "
"the **Colours ▾** of the Space, as before."
msgstr ""

msgid "``Tools ▸ Vocabularies…``"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``Settings… ▸ Vocabularies``, and **Edit vocabulary…** beside a value "
"term in the Inspector."
msgstr ""

msgid "``Tools ▸ Extensions…``"
msgstr ""

msgid "``Settings… ▸ Extensions``, as before."
msgstr ""

msgid "``Tools ▸ Plugins``"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``Settings… ▸ Extensions``, the section **Commands of the plugins** under"
" the extensions; the command search finds the commands too."
msgstr ""

msgid "``Tools ▸ Georeferencing…``"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``File ▸ Georeferencing…`` (next to ``Room ▸``), and the **Visibility ▾**"
" of the Space, as before."
msgstr ""

msgid "``Tools ▸ Python console… · beta``"
msgstr ""

msgid "``Window ▸ Python console… · beta``."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The command search calls the Settings *Preferences* when it says where a "
"command lives: ``Preferences ▸ Colours`` is the page **Colours** of "
"**Settings…**."
msgstr ""

msgid "The Window menu"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Window** holds what arranges the window rather than the study:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Workspaces ▸** lists the workspaces of the bar, the active one marked; "
"a click brings one to the front."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Scripting workspace** brings the workspace of the Python console and "
"the Script Editor."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Python console… · beta** opens the console (see :doc:`/how-"
"to/extensions/python-console`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Detach the active window** puts the active window in a window of the "
"system of its own, for another screen; **Bring back the detached "
"windows** puts them all back."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Maximise the area** spreads the active area over the whole workspace, "
"and back (:kbd:`⌃Space`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Area headers** shows or hides the headers of the areas, for this "
"session."
msgstr ""

msgid "How to read the tables"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A command that exists on some platforms only is drawn only there. In the "
"tables below, the **Where** column says where each command appears: "
"*everywhere*, a list of platforms, *desktop only*, or *macOS menu bar* "
"for the commands that the Mac also shows in the system's menu bar. :doc"
":`desktop-and-web` collects every difference between the desktop and the "
"web in one table."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The keys are given as the Mac shows them (⌘) and as Windows and Linux "
"show them (Ctrl). Some keys work in the page and are not written beside a"
" menu command: ``Edit ▸ Undo`` and ``Edit ▸ Redo`` answer to :kbd:`⌘Z` "
"and :kbd:`⇧⌘Z` (:kbd:`Ctrl+Z`, :kbd:`Ctrl+Shift+Z`), as :doc:`shortcuts` "
"lists. On the Mac, the **Edit** menu of the system's menu bar holds the "
"usual commands of text editing (Undo, Redo, Cut, Copy, Paste, Select "
"All), which act on the field being typed in."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **What it does** column repeats the description that EMStudio shows "
"when the pointer rests on the command. Three commands of **Help** have "
"none, because their names say it:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Workspaces…** opens the table of the seven workspaces, with the "
"question each answers and its windows (see :doc:`workspaces`);"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Shortcuts** opens the list of every key and gesture, with a search "
"field (see :doc:`shortcuts`);"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**EM legend** opens the legend of the shapes and lines of the Extended "
"Matrix (see :doc:`em-legend`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Where you work: the mode, the node, the room"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The chip's menu open under the identity chip at the top right: the "
"sections Author, Node and account, Where you work, and My space."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The way you are working (a file of this computer, a live link with "
"another application, a room of a node) is not a menu of the bar. It has "
"one place, the section **Where you work** of the chip's menu, the chip at"
" the top right of the window: click the label at the left of the status "
"bar, or press :kbd:`⇧⌘L` (:kbd:`Ctrl+Shift+L`), and the menu opens at "
"that section. Under **Mode** it offers three choices, which correspond to"
" the three verbs of this manual (see :doc:`/explanation/open-enter-"
"create`):"
msgstr ""

msgid "**On this computer**: a file you *open* from the disk;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**With an app**: the live link with EM Tools in Blender (or another "
"application that synchronises with EMStudio), the link the older versions"
" called *Sidecar*;"
msgstr ""

msgid "**In a room**: a room you *enter* on a StratiGraph node."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Under **Synchronised apps** the section lists the applications linked to "
"EMStudio, with **Find** to look for EM Tools on this computer. Inside a "
"room, under **Room**, it names the room and offers **Room settings…**, "
"**Open on the node**, **Leave the room**, **Resources of the room…** and "
"**Open this room in…**, which opens the same room in the other editor. "
"The node, your account on it and your rooms are in the other sections of "
"the same menu, **Node and account** and **My space** (see :doc:`/how-"
"to/rooms/sign-in`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The same section says which EMStudio service answers: its address, its "
"level and the version of s3Dgraphy, or that nobody answers at that "
"address. The label at the left of the status bar says it too, in its "
"tooltip."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The table at the end of this page, **Mode**, lists the two gestures of a "
"room that the menu uses and that stay hidden in the page."
msgstr ""

